<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castel Nuovo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1279">1279</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Maschio Angioino</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Castel Nuovo is a castle on the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, begun in 1279 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles I of Anjou</a> when he moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>. It was rebuilt in the fifteenth century by Alfonso V of Aragon, whose triumphal entrance arch, celebrating his entry into the city in 1443, is among the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> monuments in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It served as the royal residence of the Angevin and Aragonese kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castel-nuovo.jpg" alt="Castel Nuovo" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Little john at Italian Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Municip.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Castel Nuovo, often called the Maschio Angioino, stands in front of Piazza Municipio and the city hall in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Charles I of Anjou, moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> to Naples and wanted a new castle near the sea, because the Norman Castel Capuano was judged inadequate as a royal residence. The French architect Pierre de Chaulnes designed it, and building began in 1279 and took only three years. The king never lived there. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">War of the Sicilian Vespers</a> cost the House of Anjou the crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, the new palace stood unused until the death of Charles I in 1285.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Charles II moved in with his court and enlarged it. On 13 December 1294 Pope Celestine V abdicated in its main hall, and the cardinals elected Boniface VIII there on 24 December. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-king-of-naples">Robert, King of Naples</a>, from 1309, it became a centre of culture, hosting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Boccaccio</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a> painted the Palatine Chapel in 1332. Joanna I fled in 1347 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a> advanced to avenge the death of her husband Andrew, and his army looted the castle. A legend says that Joanna II had her lovers thrown through a trapdoor to sea monsters, and another tells of a crocodile in the dungeons.</p><p>Alfonso V of Aragon set up his court in the castle in 1443 and had it rebuilt in Catalan Gothic style by Guillem Sagrera, with five round towers suited to the guns of the time. Its main gate became a marble triumphal arch commemorating his entry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1443, which modern authors attribute to Francesco Laurana, and the work was finished in 1479. In the Hall of the Barons, Ferdinand I had barons who had conspired against him arrested at a wedding feast, and some were put to death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-viii-of-france">Charles VIII of France</a> looted the castle in 1494, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> it was a garrison and the viceroy's residence until the early 17th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Italian state took the castle for civil purposes, and restoration lasted until 1939. The castle holds a civic museum, opened in 1990, which includes the Palatine Chapel, the only surviving element of the 14th-century castle of the House of Anjou, and the Neapolitan Society of Homeland History has its headquarters and library there. The Hall of the Barons, 26 by 28 metres, has an octagonal star vault, and until 2006 it hosted the city council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. The triumphal arch is 35 metres tall, with a statue of Saint Michael at its top.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Castel Nuovo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles shifted the centre of government from Palermo to Naples, where he built the <b>Castel Nuovo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
